== Title/identity audit ==

Two generic titles required normalization during capture.

'''Discourse''' was already occupied by the canonical public knowledge object for the Discourse forum software. The linguistic concept is therefore [[Discourse (linguistics)]]. No second <code>Discourse</code> identity was created.

'''Context''' is substantially broader than linguistic interpretation and even within pragmatics names multiple contextual notions. The wave therefore uses [[Context (linguistics)]] and leaves the unqualified title available for future general/disambiguation use.

[[Reference]] remains unqualified because its Core article defines the broader representational relation while treating linguistic reference as the central Wave 3 case.

== Language audit ==

[[Language]]:

* includes spoken and signed modalities;
* does not equate language with speech or writing;
* treats the ontology/object of linguistic study as theoretically contested;
* separates natural language from deliberately specified formal/programming languages;
* avoids canonizing one generative, usage-based, cognitive, social, or structural school as the definition of language.

== Syntax audit ==

[[Syntax]]:

* concerns structural combination;
* is separated from semantics and morphology;
* presents constituency, dependency, generative, constraint-based, functional, constructional and usage-based approaches as frameworks rather than definitions;
* separates grammaticality/acceptability from raw frequency;
* distinguishes natural-language syntax from formal-language syntax.

== Pragmatics/context audit ==

[[Pragmatics]] keeps the semantics–pragmatics boundary explicitly theory-sensitive.

[[Context (linguistics)]] distinguishes:

* linguistic co-text;
* utterance parameters;
* discourse/information state;
* perceptual/social circumstances;
* common ground.

The articles therefore do not use "context" as an unexplained catch-all.

[[Common ground]] is modeled as mutually available/accepted information, commitments or related states, not as objective truth or all shared knowledge.

== Reference audit ==

[[Reference]] separates:

<pre>
reference ≠ meaning
reference ≠ identity
identifier ≠ guaranteed referent
co-reference ≠ synonymy
</pre>

The Technical surface distinguishes model-theoretic denotation from community/world reference and preserves the strength of formal identity assertions such as <code>owl:sameAs</code> without importing Wave 2 ontology implementation into the definition.

== Ambiguity/polysemy audit ==

[[Ambiguity]] distinguishes lexical, structural and scope ambiguity and separates ambiguity from vagueness.

[[Polysemy]] is treated as related-sense multiplicity, not as all lexical ambiguity.

The polysemy/homonymy boundary remains theory- and evidence-sensitive rather than being presented as mechanically decidable.

== Compositionality audit ==

[[Compositionality]]:

* requires structure as well as constituent meanings;
* is not reduced to word-by-word substitution;
* does not imply context independence;
* treats idioms, coercion, anaphora, quotation and pragmatic enrichment as challenges to specific formulations rather than automatic refutations.

== Presupposition/implicature audit ==

[[Presupposition]] distinguishes backgrounded information, projection and accommodation from ordinary entailment.

[[Implicature]] distinguishes speaker implicating from hearer inferring and treats conversational implicature as defeasible.

The articles do not collapse presupposition, implicature and entailment into a generic category of "implied meaning."

== Speech-act audit ==

[[Speech act]] separates:

* sentence form;
* locutionary content;
* illocutionary force;
* perlocutionary effect.

Felicity conditions and institutional authority are presented as part of act success rather than grammar.

== Discourse audit ==

[[Discourse (linguistics)]] covers connected spoken, signed, written and multimodal language use.

It does not equate discourse with text, a sentence sequence, one methodology, or the [[Discourse]] software project.

Formal discourse semantics, conversation analysis, interactional approaches and computational discourse are presented as distinct research traditions.

== Lexical-semantics audit ==

[[Lexical semantics]] distinguishes lexical meaning from dictionary glosses and lexicography.

It presents sense relations and the lexical representation problem without treating a dictionary sense inventory as a ground-truth ontology.

== Distributional-semantics audit ==

[[Distributional semantics]] explicitly preserves:

<pre>
co-occurrence ≠ reference
vector proximity ≠ synonymy
embedding dimension ≠ semantic feature by default
distributional semantics ≠ complete theory of meaning
</pre>

Static vectors, count-based spaces, learned embeddings and contextual representations are treated as related distributional model families.

== Semantic-similarity audit ==

[[Semantic similarity]] distinguishes:

* similarity;
* broader semantic relatedness/association;
* synonymy;
* identity.

Cosine similarity is described as a geometric measure over a representation, not as semantics itself.

Sentence/text similarity is treated as requiring more than lexical overlap.

== Meaning-representation audit ==

[[Meaning representation]] distinguishes linguistic-content representation from [[Knowledge representation]].

Logical forms, discourse representations and graph formalisms are examples rather than universal standards.

AMR is not described as “the” universal meaning representation.

Dense embeddings and explicit symbolic structures are distinguished while allowing hybrid systems.

== Public concept versus BITwiki application ==

The wave preserves:

<pre>
Language                 ≠ prompt language / house style
Context (linguistics)    ≠ agent context window
Discourse (linguistics)  ≠ Discourse software
Common ground            ≠ BIThub shared state
Meaning representation   ≠ BITwiki schema
Semantic similarity      ≠ BITwiki link strength
Distributional semantics ≠ BITwiki embedding implementation
</pre>

No local agent or BITwiki architecture was used as the definition of a public concept.

